{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Added one more deterministic case aimed at the entry that actually opened the joint configuration in the finding's history. Applying a JointImplicit change whose only change removes node 4 - a node that is not a member of the voter set - leaves the node in a joint configuration: the draft-side run reports joint=true, auto_leave=true, voters still [1,2,3] and outgoing_size == incoming_size == 3. So a configuration change with no effect on membership still enters joint consensus, which means it also requires a leave-joint entry to exit, and by the knowledge recorded two turns ago that leave is proposed only by a node that is leader when it applies the change (raft.go:742). Together those give the concrete path the finding's history took: the first change was a no-op joint entry whose proposer did not remain leader long enough to leave the configuration, so no leave was ever appended, and the next leader's guard - seeing a non-joint tracker and a pendingConfIndex that predates the committed change - admitted a second joint-enter change, which every node applying the log in order then rejects with the Changer's 'config is already joint', a panic by contract. The case is requested for isolated execution so the no-op-enters-joint behaviour is receipt-backed, and the request states its limits: the change is applied directly rather than proposed, so the case establishes the resulting configuration state for that change, not how a cluster reaches it. That makes this the last mechanism link not already carried by a receipt; the asserted availability claim, its earlier receipts and its recorded counterevidence and limitations are unchanged.\"}"}}
